1. What is Network Security?
Network security involves policies, configurations, and tools that protect the integrity, confidentiality, and availability of data. In the context of CCNA, you'll focus on:

Access Control Lists (ACLs)
Port Security
Secure Protocols
2. Access Control Lists (ACLs)
An ACL is a set of rules that control network traffic by allowing or denying specific traffic based on criteria such as source/destination IP, protocol, or port.

Types of ACLs
Standard ACLs:

Filters traffic based on the source IP address only.
Uses numbers 1–99 and 1300–1999.
Applied close to the destination.
Extended ACLs:

Filters traffic based on source/destination IP, protocols, and ports.
Uses numbers 100–199 and 2000–2699.
Applied close to the source.
Standard ACL Configuration
Scenario: You want to block traffic from 192.168.1.10 to a network.

Create the ACL:
  Router> enable
  Router# configure terminal
  Router(config)# access-list 1 deny 192.168.1.10
  Router(config)# access-list 1 permit any

Apply the ACL to an Interface:
  Router(config)# interface GigabitEthernet 0/0
  Router(config-if)# ip access-group 1 in
  Router(config-if)# exit

Verify ACL:
  Router# show access-lists

Extended ACL Configuration
Scenario: You want to block HTTP traffic from 192.168.1.0/24 to 192.168.2.0/24.

Create the ACL:
  Router> enable
  Router# configure terminal
  Router(config)# access-list 100 deny tcp 192.168.1.0 0.0.0.255 192.168.2.0 0.0.0.255 eq 80
  Router(config)# access-list 100 permit ip any any

Apply the ACL to an Interface:
  Router(config)# interface GigabitEthernet 0/0
  Router(config-if)# ip access-group 100 in
  Router(config-if)# exit

Verify ACL:
  Router# show access-lists

3. Port Security
Port Security limits the devices that can connect to a switch port, based on their MAC addresses.

Port Security Configuration:
Enable Port Security on a Port:
  Switch> enable
  Switch# configure terminal
  Switch(config)# interface FastEthernet 0/1
  Switch(config-if)# switchport mode access
  Switch(config-if)# switchport port-security

Set Maximum MAC Addresses:
  Switch(config-if)# switchport port-security maximum 2

Add Static MAC Addresses:
  Switch(config-if)# switchport port-security mac-address 00:11:22:33:44:55
Set Violation Action (Optional):

Protect: Drops packets from unknown MACs but does not log.
Restrict: Drops packets and logs the violation.
Shutdown: Disables the port.
  Switch(config-if)# switchport port-security violation shutdown

Verify Configuration:
  Switch# show port-security

4. Secure Network Protocols
1)SSH (Secure Shell):

Replaces Telnet for secure remote management.
Example configuration:
  Router> enable
  Router# configure terminal
  Router(config)# hostname Router1
  Router(config)# ip domain-name example.com
  Router(config)# crypto key generate rsa
  Router(config)# username admin privilege 15 secret cisco123
  Router(config)# line vty 0 4
  Router(config-line)# transport input ssh
  Router(config-line)# login local
  Router(config-line)# exit

2)HTTPS:
Secures web-based management interfaces.

3)Syslog:
Collects and stores logs for network monitoring.

4)NTP (Network Time Protocol):
Synchronizes time across network devices.

